feat(join): show what a community's directory will show about you before you ask to join (#357)

The join manifest is now asked for at vtc/join-requests/manifest/0.4, which carries the community's directory disclosure statement (VTI-DIR-130): what its member directory would show about the person joining, and to whom. A community that predates 0.4 refuses it with unsupportedType, and the same question is asked again at 0.3, which carries no statement.

The join screen shows the statement in plain sentences addressed to the person, before Send request:
- what a new card shows and to whom;
- the widest each field can go;
- what the community sets itself;
- whether members can find them;
- how far their DID can reach;
- whether notes are kept, whether groups exist, and whether a card can leave the community.

Field labels are the community's own words, rendered as text.

A statement the wallet cannot show faithfully stops the join before anything about the person is sent, rather than letting them apply without being told.

task-surface.json is re-synced against the vta-sdk that exports manifest 0.4 (VTI feat/vtc-directory-disclosure).

Signed-off-by: Glenn Gore <glenn.g@affinidi.com>

A community offering a member directory must publish a statement of what the directory discloses, and to whom, generated from its configuration, and show it to applicants before they apply (VTI spec **VTI-DIR-130**). No Trust Task carried one, which VTI recorded as a divergence (trustoverip/dtgwg-vti-spec#69). This adds the task and puts the statement where applicants already look.

### `vtc/directory/disclosure/0.1` (new)
- **Request:** the payload is empty. The task answers every reader the community answers `vtc/join-requests/manifest` for, with or without a proof:
  - a community that publishes its join requirements to anyone answers this to anyone;
  - one that publishes them only to callers it can identify may do the same here, and must not be stricter.
- **Response:** the community's DID and a `DirectoryDisclosure`, defined in `directory/_shared/0.1/disclosure-statement.schema.json`. It contains:
  - every active field, with its `source`, `ceiling`, `defaultVisibility`, `searchable` and `facet`. Disabled and retired fields are left out;
  - `newCard`: the listing and search setting a new card starts with, plus the introductions setting where the community delivers introductions;
  - `membershipIdentifiers`: how far the DID a member joined with can reach (the `did` field's ceiling, or `admins` when that field is off);
  - `adminNotes`, whether administrators keep notes, which VTI-DIR-033 makes part of the statement;
  - `groups`: present only where the community has groups, with the validity of each group membership credential;
  - `externalCards`: whether a member's card can be published outside the community;
  - `statementDigest`: RFC 8785 JCS of the statement without the digest, as a SHA-256 multihash in base58btc, so an applicant can record which statement it saw. The statement has no timestamp, so the digest changes exactly when its content changes.
- **Rules:**
  - every audience in the statement is the widest a value can reach, because policy only narrows (VTI-DIR-021);
  - the statement is never tailored to the reader;
  - the community regenerates it on every read.

### `vtc/join-requests/manifest/0.4`
0.4 adds one optional response member, `directoryDisclosure`, holding the same statement. Nothing else changes from 0.3.
- **When it is present:** exactly where the community offers a directory.
- **Digests:** it sits outside every criterion, so no `requirementsDigest` changes.
- **Applicants** should show the statement before submitting, and record its digest with the application.

### Generated and checked
- **Bindings:** regenerated in Rust, TS, Go and Dart. `check-bindings` agrees across all four.
- **Examples:** the digests in the examples are the real values, computed independently. VTI's implementation recomputes the manifest example's digest in a test.
- **Local runs:**
  - `npm run validate`, then `cargo test --workspace --all-targets`: 1867 passed;
  - conformance (`disclosure` / `manifest`): 26 passed;
  - clippy `-D warnings`: clean;
  - TS `tsc`, `npm test` and smoke: pass;
  - Go build, vet and test: pass;
  - Dart analyze and test: pass.

The release is additive: new modules only, so a patch release.

docs(vtc): data-rooms quickstart re-run end to end, with S3 direct transfer (#2122)

Re-ran the quickstart from zero on main (7882a7f1) and rewrote it from what
the run printed:

- a separate community VTA (C) for the VTC, since a member's VTA cannot yet
  reach a VTC that speaks DIDComm;
- the pnm build split in two (features apply to every package of a build);
- a headless alternative to the console for the first administrator and for
  admitting a member;
- a scoped (application-role) client for Bob's backfill and writes;
- group renewal from the owner's VTA, and `pnm rooms renew` refusing a
  sealed room;
- what a removed member actually sees;
- section 10: S3 direct transfer against LocalStack — local mediator, bucket,
  external account at the community VTA, storage config and assignment,
  multipart upload and download, and what changes for real S3;
- section 11: room rate limits, a 429 and the client's read retry;
- "What does not work yet" refreshed: the transport-selection and probe
  blockers this run found, the lifecycle rule's prefix, presign batch size.

Signed-off-by: Glenn Gore <glenn.g@affinidi.com>

Implements trustoverip/dtgwg-trust-tasks-tf#773 (trust-tasks-rs 0.31.4): renew and the CLI. `rooms/keys/act`'s `sealedRoomMint` (recipient item 9) landed separately in #2110.

## Why
A sealed room's (`attributed` / `private`) epoch is its MLS group's epoch plus one. A bare `rooms/epoch/mint` moves the host and no member: every write is then refused as sealed under a superseded epoch, and the owner's next `group/{add,remove}` fails `epochConflict`. Until now a sealed room whose membership stayed the same had no correct way to renew. It also had one way to brick itself that looked like a renewal: `pnm rooms renew`.

## What
- **`rooms/owner/group/renew/0.1`** on the VTA. It is the third advance beside add and remove, on the same remote-first `HostCall` machinery (settle → admin presentation → advance):
  - Item 1: `noGroup`, plus `noAuthority` before anything is built.
  - Items 2/3/7: `SealedRoom::renew` (new, in vti-rooms) builds a self-update commit and the epoch link on a copy. `room_groups::prepare_renew` keeps them durably as the room's one pending advance, of the new kind `AdvanceKind::Renewal`, before anything is sent.
  - Items 4/5: mint with the commit, the link, the owner persona's `admin` presentation and the reason, signed as the persona. The advance is applied on acceptance and discarded on refusal (`hostRefused`, or `epochConflict` + `hostEpoch`). On an unknown outcome it is kept (`hostUnreachable`).
  - Item 6: any pending admission, removal or renewal is settled via `rooms/info` first.
- SDK: `TASK_ROOMS_OWNER_GROUP_RENEW_0_1` and `VtaClient::room_group_renew`, using the generated types. Retry safety is `Keyed`, because a re-execution would renew twice. The task is added to the outbound budget, classed `Sensitive` in the vta-mcp guard, and has a conformance census entry.
- CLI: `pnm rooms owner group-renew --room --host-did [--reason]` runs inside `idempotent`. Its output tells the owner that members catch up with `pnm rooms keys commit --host`. `pnm rooms renew` reads the tier first and refuses `attributed` / `private` (act producer item 4), naming `group-renew`.
- No dependency change: main is already on trust-tasks-rs 0.31.4 (#2110), which carries renew. This PR touches neither `Cargo.toml` nor `Cargo.lock`.
- Docs: data-rooms.md §10.1 (how each tier renews) and the refusal table, the quickstart, and the files design-note P0 row.

## Tests
- vta-service `room_owner_group`:
  - the host advances first, then the group;
  - the remaining member applies the commit and reads the new records, and earlier records still open through the link;
  - membership is unchanged;
  - `noGroup` and `noAuthority`;
  - a refusal leaves the group state untouched;
  - another admin's advance gives `epochConflict` with `hostEpoch`;
  - a lost reply is kept as a `Renewal` and the next renewal settles it;
  - a pending renewal the host never took is discarded;
  - one pending advance per room across add, remove and renew, in every settle direction, and the slot refuses a second;
  - per-persona isolation: another persona on the same VTA keeps its own group until it applies the commit;
  - the mint is signed as the owner persona.
- vti-rooms: `SealedRoom::renew` round trip.
- vta-cli-common: the `pnm rooms renew` guard.

## Local checks
fmt, workspace clippy `-D warnings`, the reduced-feature vta-service check and clippy runs, vti-rooms `mls` clippy, workspace nextest, doctests, the vtc nextest and doctests (3122 passed), and `cargo deny` all pass. Under load, workspace nextest had two 300 s timeouts: `audit_coverage::every_consequential_task_records_an_audit_entry` (the known flake) and `vta-backup every_backed_up_keyspace_survives_a_round_trip` (Argon2-bound). Both pass when re-run alone, in 55 s and 227 s.

Design note, **accepted** 2026-10-15: `docs/05-design-notes/witness-host-service.md`.

A witness is an independent **host service** (`spec/02-architecture.md`), not a VTA or VTC role. It is a standalone `vti-witness` binary in this workspace:
- its identity is provisioned from its operator's VTA through a new `witness` DID template;
- up to 10 witness identities per service;
- TSP/DIDComm only;
- an independence rule, so it refuses to witness itself or its operator;
- eligibility keyed on community membership by default.

v1 has no ZKP: the witness sees what it is presented, so disclosure is a deployment decision (§6). Implementation follows in phases P1–P3 (§4).

`vtc/oid4vp/identities/list/0.1` lets a community's configuration administrator read the OpenID4VP verifier identities the community presents to swiyu and EUDI wallets, and what stops each from serving one.

A verifier identity is installed out of band — a DID registered with an ecosystem, an access certificate from a registrar, trust statements from a registry — and each part expires on its own schedule. A wallet that refuses a request object tells the community nothing.

Per identity the response gives:
- the client identifier wallets see, the DID and signing method;
- where the key is held (`cached`, or kept by the community's `agent`);
- the access certificate's subject, issuer and validity;
- each `verifier_info` attestation with its expiry;
- whether it is the default, and whether a session could be opened on it now.

Problems use a fixed vocabulary with `error` / `warning` severity: invalid key custody, no loaded signer, no link to the agent, a missing / not-yet-valid / expired / expiring certificate, a missing identity trust statement, an expired or expiring statement; and, community-wide, a default identity that is not installed, or no https public URL.

The response never carries key material, nor the identifier under which the agent holds a key.

Bindings regenerated (Rust, TS, Go, Dart); `npm run validate`, `npm run check-bindings` and `cargo test -p trust-tasks-rs --all-features` pass.

Consumer: OpenVTC/verifiable-trust-infrastructure#1988 (the VTC console's Wallet verifier page).

P9, part 2 of 3. These are the host tasks behind the console's room detail and Usage pages (`docs/05-design-notes/data-rooms-files.md` §6.2–6.3, §8.1), built on the history and overrides from #2073. The specs come from `trust-tasks-rs` 0.31, so there is no spec change.

## Tasks
- **`vtc/rooms/get`** (`vtc.rooms.admin`) returns:
  - the room's host record;
  - its assigned config, plus every earlier config still holding its blobs;
  - effective limits, with each scope's source and the host ceiling;
  - usage and orphans;
  - the 10 largest blobs, by size only;
  - `membersWithUsage`, a count only, never DIDs, and omitted on private rooms.
- **`vtc/rooms/usage`** (`vtc.rooms.admin`):
  - Grouping: by room, member, storage config or day.
  - Filters: room, member, config, and an inclusive date range. The default is the last 30 days.
  - Sorting, plus a cursor tied to the query.
  - Refusals:
    - `invalidRange`: over 400 days, inverted, or starting before the oldest kept day;
    - `invalidCursor`;
    - `memberUsageUnavailable`: a member grouping or filter on a named private room.
  - Activity grouped by config is attributed to each room's current assignment.
- **`vtc/rooms/limits/set`** (`vtc.rooms.admin`) sets limits for a room, its member default, or one member.
  - It answers with the effective limits, the target scope's usage, and `overLimit`.
  - It refuses with `aboveCeiling`, `memberLimitsUnavailable` and `invalidCombination`; a malformed target gets `malformedRequest`.
  - Each change is audited as the new `RoomLimitsChanged` event (added to `AuditEvent`, which is `#[non_exhaustive]`).
  - It is not parked for approval, per the spec.
- **`rooms/usage`** is the owner's read and needs an `admin` chain. Both hosts serve it through `vti_rooms::blob_tasks::usage`. Private rooms answer `notAvailable`.
- **`vtc/rooms/list`** moves from plain admin to `vtc.rooms.admin`, as §6.2 says. Its note in the dispatched-without-proof list said "a public read", which it never was; the note is corrected.

## Supporting changes
- `routes::rooms::hosted_room` renders a single room the same way the list does.
- `TestVtc::with_room_file_ceiling` makes `aboveCeiling` reachable. The schema caps `maxFileBytes` at 1 GiB, the same as the default ceiling.
- Census entries:
  - conformance witnesses for the 3 tasks;
  - error-code witnesses for all 8 declared codes;
  - dispatched-without-proof reasons for the two reads;
  - the vti-rooms dispatched-URI registry check.

## Tests
- **New:** `vtc-service/tests/it/room_usage_reports.rs` (10 tests) covers:
  - each task's shape;
  - that private rooms disclose nothing per member;
  - that an override is enforced, audited and cleared;
  - turning files off for one room;
  - each grouping;
  - paging;
  - every declared refusal;
  - the `vtc.rooms.admin` gate on all four tasks.
- **Also new:** a room-host test for the owner's `rooms/usage`.
- **Results:**
  - `cargo nextest run -p vtc-service -p vtc-client`: 3091 passed.
  - `cargo nextest run --workspace --exclude vtc-service --exclude vtc-client`: 5742 passed.
  - `cargo clippy -p vti-common -p vti-rooms -p room-host -p vtc-service --all-targets -- -D warnings` and fmt: clean.

## Notes
- This touches `surface_tasks.rs` and `test_support.rs`, as #2075 does. Whichever merges second will need a small rebase.
- **Next, part 3:** the admin-UI pages (room detail, Storage, Usage with CSV export, Settings ▸ Rooms).
